1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,080
If you were a teenager in nineteen

2
00:00:02,080 --> 00:00:05,200
fifty-nine, you were living through something brand

3
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:05,445
new.

4
00:00:05,625 --> 00:00:08,101
You were part of the first generation

5
00:00:08,101 --> 00:00:10,377
in history to be called teenagers,

6
00:00:10,377 --> 00:00:11,716
with your own music,

7
00:00:11,716 --> 00:00:12,720
your own money,

8
00:00:12,720 --> 00:00:15,331
your own clothes and your own language.

9
00:00:15,511 --> 00:00:17,388
Grown-ups didn't quite know what to make

10
00:00:17,388 --> 00:00:17,716
of you.

11
00:00:17,896 --> 00:00:20,625
And honestly, you didn't always know either.

12
00:00:20,975 --> 00:00:22,601
In nineteen fifty-nine,

13
00:00:22,601 --> 00:00:26,348
there were about eighteen million American teenagers,

14
00:00:26,348 --> 00:00:28,752
and the number was growing fast as

15
00:00:28,752 --> 00:00:31,226
the oldest baby boomers got closer.

16
00:00:31,406 --> 00:00:34,651
Businesses had just discovered that teenagers had

17
00:00:34,651 --> 00:00:35,644
money to spend.

18
00:00:35,824 --> 00:00:38,222
Magazines, record companies,

19
00:00:38,222 --> 00:00:41,819
soda makers and clothing stores all wanted

20
00:00:41,819 --> 00:00:43,103
your attention.

21
00:00:43,454 --> 00:00:45,572
So let's spend a week as a

22
00:00:45,572 --> 00:00:48,179
teenager in nineteen fifty-nine.

23
00:00:48,359 --> 00:00:51,124
From Monday morning in homeroom to Saturday

24
00:00:51,124 --> 00:00:52,538
night at the drive-in.

25
00:00:52,718 --> 00:00:53,828
If you were there,

26
00:00:53,828 --> 00:00:56,233
I think you'll feel like you're sixteen

27
00:00:56,233 --> 00:00:57,281
again by the end.

28
00:00:57,631 --> 00:00:59,917
Monday morning started with a fight over

29
00:00:59,917 --> 00:01:00,661
the bathroom.

30
00:01:00,841 --> 00:01:02,790
Most houses had just one,

31
00:01:02,790 --> 00:01:05,206
and a family with four kids had

32
00:01:05,206 --> 00:01:06,297
to take turns.

33
00:01:06,477 --> 00:01:08,580
Girls had set their hair in pin

34
00:01:08,580 --> 00:01:10,887
curls or rollers the night before,

35
00:01:10,887 --> 00:01:12,108
and slept on them.

36
00:01:12,288 --> 00:01:15,529
Boys combed their hair with Brylcreem or

37
00:01:15,529 --> 00:01:16,178
Vitalis.

38
00:01:16,358 --> 00:01:18,047
A little dab will do ya,

39
00:01:18,047 --> 00:01:19,666
as the commercial said.

40
00:01:20,017 --> 00:01:22,924
Girls wore full skirts or straight skirts

41
00:01:22,924 --> 00:01:23,987
below the knee,

42
00:01:23,987 --> 00:01:26,611
with a blouse and a cardigan sweater.

43
00:01:26,791 --> 00:01:29,392
Pants were usually not allowed at school.

44
00:01:29,572 --> 00:01:30,612
On their feet,

45
00:01:30,612 --> 00:01:32,843
saddle shoes or penny loafers,

46
00:01:32,843 --> 00:01:34,552
with white bobby socks.

47
00:01:34,732 --> 00:01:36,653
Some still wore a poodle skirt,

48
00:01:36,653 --> 00:01:39,132
though by fifty-nine they were going out

49
00:01:39,132 --> 00:01:39,690
of style.

50
00:01:40,039 --> 00:01:42,373
Boys wore slacks with a belt,

51
00:01:42,373 --> 00:01:45,512
a button-down shirt and maybe a sweater

52
00:01:45,512 --> 00:01:45,914
vest.

53
00:01:46,094 --> 00:01:48,175
Blue jeans were for weekends,

54
00:01:48,175 --> 00:01:50,901
and many schools banned them in class.

55
00:01:51,081 --> 00:01:53,340
A boy who wore his jeans rolled

56
00:01:53,340 --> 00:01:55,673
at the cuff with a white T-shirt

57
00:01:55,673 --> 00:01:58,078
and a leather jacket was making a

58
00:01:58,078 --> 00:02:00,775
statement, and the principal noticed.

59
00:02:01,124 --> 00:02:02,425
Breakfast was quick.

60
00:02:02,605 --> 00:02:04,021
Cereal with milk,

61
00:02:04,021 --> 00:02:06,771
toast, or a glass of orange juice

62
00:02:06,771 --> 00:02:09,188
made from frozen concentrate.

63
00:02:09,368 --> 00:02:10,610
Then out the door.

64
00:02:10,790 --> 00:02:13,466
Most teenagers walked to school,

65
00:02:13,466 --> 00:02:16,142
or rode a big yellow school bus.

66
00:02:16,322 --> 00:02:18,459
A lucky few had a friend with

67
00:02:18,459 --> 00:02:20,743
a car, and that friend was very

68
00:02:20,743 --> 00:02:21,848
popular indeed.

69
00:02:22,199 --> 00:02:25,291
High school in nineteen fifty-nine was more

70
00:02:25,291 --> 00:02:26,586
crowded than ever,

71
00:02:26,586 --> 00:02:28,096
and more serious too.

72
00:02:28,276 --> 00:02:31,048
In October of nineteen fifty-seven,

73
00:02:31,048 --> 00:02:34,058
the Soviet Union had launched Sputnik.

74
00:02:34,238 --> 00:02:37,189
Suddenly, the country was worried that American

75
00:02:37,189 --> 00:02:39,010
students were falling behind.

76
00:02:39,190 --> 00:02:42,903
Congress passed the National Defense Education Act

77
00:02:42,903 --> 00:02:46,245
in fifty-eight, and schools poured money into

78
00:02:46,245 --> 00:02:48,919
science, math and foreign languages.

79
00:02:49,269 --> 00:02:50,743
Teachers were strict.

80
00:02:50,923 --> 00:02:52,463
You stood up when you answered a

81
00:02:52,463 --> 00:02:52,897
question.

82
00:02:53,077 --> 00:02:55,259
You called them Mister and Missus.

83
00:02:55,439 --> 00:02:57,304
Chewing gum got you sent to the

84
00:02:57,304 --> 00:02:58,446
principal's office.

85
00:02:58,626 --> 00:03:01,459
And many schools still allowed paddling.

86
00:03:01,639 --> 00:03:03,390
Homework was done by hand,

87
00:03:03,390 --> 00:03:05,747
with a fountain pen or a ballpoint,

88
00:03:05,747 --> 00:03:08,373
and you'd better have neat handwriting.

89
00:03:08,723 --> 00:03:10,165
Boys took shop class,

90
00:03:10,165 --> 00:03:12,089
where they built birdhouses,

91
00:03:12,089 --> 00:03:15,387
bookshelves and sometimes worked on car engines.

92
00:03:15,567 --> 00:03:17,277
Girls took home economics,

93
00:03:17,277 --> 00:03:19,054
where they learned to cook,

94
00:03:19,054 --> 00:03:21,093
sew and run a household budget.

95
00:03:21,273 --> 00:03:23,542
It was assumed that most girls would

96
00:03:23,542 --> 00:03:25,685
marry young, and many of them did.

97
00:03:26,035 --> 00:03:28,026
Lunch was in the cafeteria,

98
00:03:28,026 --> 00:03:28,763
on a tray.

99
00:03:28,943 --> 00:03:31,993
Sloppy joes, fish sticks on Friday,

100
00:03:31,993 --> 00:03:35,217
mashed potatoes, a carton of milk and

101
00:03:35,217 --> 00:03:36,698
a square of cake.

102
00:03:36,878 --> 00:03:38,552
Or you brought your lunch in a

103
00:03:38,552 --> 00:03:39,444
brown paper bag.

104
00:03:39,624 --> 00:03:41,181
Tables were divided,

105
00:03:41,181 --> 00:03:43,049
almost by unwritten law.

106
00:03:43,229 --> 00:03:45,112
The athletes, the brains,

107
00:03:45,112 --> 00:03:47,974
the greasers and the popular kids each

108
00:03:47,974 --> 00:03:49,104
had their spot.

109
00:03:49,454 --> 00:03:50,882
And then there were the clubs.

110
00:03:51,062 --> 00:03:53,402
Glee club, the school paper,

111
00:03:53,402 --> 00:03:55,909
the yearbook, the debate team,

112
00:03:55,909 --> 00:03:59,837
Future Farmers of America and Future Homemakers

113
00:03:59,837 --> 00:04:00,756
of America.

114
00:04:00,936 --> 00:04:03,479
Almost everyone belonged to something.

115
00:04:03,659 --> 00:04:06,245
Pictures from those clubs fill the old

116
00:04:06,245 --> 00:04:09,036
yearbooks, rows of serious young faces in

117
00:04:09,036 --> 00:04:10,669
crew cuts and ponytails,

118
00:04:10,669 --> 00:04:12,916
all of them certain the world was

119
00:04:12,916 --> 00:04:14,073
waiting for them.

120
00:04:14,423 --> 00:04:17,818
After school, many teenagers went straight to

121
00:04:17,818 --> 00:04:18,196
work.

122
00:04:18,376 --> 00:04:20,396
A part-time job was normal.

123
00:04:20,576 --> 00:04:22,781
Boys delivered newspapers,

124
00:04:22,781 --> 00:04:25,240
bagged groceries, pumped gas,

125
00:04:25,240 --> 00:04:27,275
or worked as soda jerks.

126
00:04:27,455 --> 00:04:31,215
Girls babysat, worked at the five-and-dime,

127
00:04:31,215 --> 00:04:32,877
or answered phones.

128
00:04:33,057 --> 00:04:36,278
The minimum wage in nineteen fifty-nine was

129
00:04:36,278 --> 00:04:37,701
one dollar an hour.

130
00:04:38,051 --> 00:04:39,885
And with that money came freedom.

131
00:04:40,065 --> 00:04:42,735
Teenagers spent it on records,

132
00:04:42,735 --> 00:04:44,871
movies, clothes, makeup,

133
00:04:44,871 --> 00:04:47,008
hamburgers and gasoline.

134
00:04:47,188 --> 00:04:48,930
By the end of the fifties,

135
00:04:48,930 --> 00:04:52,347
American teenagers were spending around ten billion

136
00:04:52,347 --> 00:04:53,353
dollars a year.

137
00:04:53,533 --> 00:04:56,377
That was more than many entire industries.

138
00:04:56,557 --> 00:04:59,740
Businesses noticed, and the teenage market was

139
00:04:59,740 --> 00:05:00,086
born.

140
00:05:00,437 --> 00:05:01,581
If you didn't work,

141
00:05:01,581 --> 00:05:02,364
you hung out.

142
00:05:02,544 --> 00:05:04,856
At the drugstore soda fountain,

143
00:05:04,856 --> 00:05:06,871
sitting on spinning stools,

144
00:05:06,871 --> 00:05:09,556
sharing a cherry Coke or a chocolate

145
00:05:09,556 --> 00:05:10,079
malted.

146
00:05:10,259 --> 00:05:11,493
At the local diner,

147
00:05:11,493 --> 00:05:14,091
where the jukebox played three songs for

148
00:05:14,091 --> 00:05:14,740
a quarter.

149
00:05:14,920 --> 00:05:16,747
Or just at someone's house,

150
00:05:16,747 --> 00:05:19,317
sitting on the front steps and talking

151
00:05:19,317 --> 00:05:19,994
for hours.

152
00:05:20,343 --> 00:05:22,062
Music was everything.

153
00:05:22,242 --> 00:05:25,435
And nineteen fifty-nine began with a heartbreak.

154
00:05:25,615 --> 00:05:27,067
On February third,

155
00:05:27,067 --> 00:05:29,649
a small plane crashed in a snowy

156
00:05:29,649 --> 00:05:31,102
cornfield in Iowa,

157
00:05:31,102 --> 00:05:32,716
killing Buddy Holly,

158
00:05:32,716 --> 00:05:35,460
Ritchie Valens and the Big Bopper.

159
00:05:35,640 --> 00:05:37,652
They were on a winter tour of

160
00:05:37,652 --> 00:05:38,484
the Midwest.

161
00:05:38,664 --> 00:05:41,435
Years later, Don McLean would call it

162
00:05:41,435 --> 00:05:43,157
the day the music died.

163
00:05:43,507 --> 00:05:45,121
But the music didn't die.

164
00:05:45,301 --> 00:05:48,529
Nineteen fifty-nine was full of hits.

165
00:05:48,709 --> 00:05:51,115
Johnny Horton's The Battle of New Orleans

166
00:05:51,115 --> 00:05:53,052
was the biggest song of the year,

167
00:05:53,052 --> 00:05:55,165
and Bobby Darin's Mack the Knife was

168
00:05:55,165 --> 00:05:56,104
right behind it.

169
00:05:56,284 --> 00:05:59,025
There was Lloyd Price's Stagger Lee,

170
00:05:59,025 --> 00:06:01,917
The Platters' Smoke Gets in Your Eyes,

171
00:06:01,917 --> 00:06:03,668
Paul Anka's Lonely Boy,

172
00:06:03,668 --> 00:06:06,484
and Dion and the Belmonts' A Teenager

173
00:06:06,484 --> 00:06:07,093
in Love.

174
00:06:07,273 --> 00:06:09,584
Every song seemed to be about you.

175
00:06:09,933 --> 00:06:11,497
Elvis was in the Army,

176
00:06:11,497 --> 00:06:12,989
stationed in Germany,

177
00:06:12,989 --> 00:06:15,476
and his fans counted the days until

178
00:06:15,476 --> 00:06:16,400
he came home.

179
00:06:16,580 --> 00:06:18,808
Meanwhile, every afternoon,

180
00:06:18,808 --> 00:06:22,852
millions of teenagers watched American Bandstand,

181
00:06:22,852 --> 00:06:24,585
hosted by Dick Clark,

182
00:06:24,585 --> 00:06:26,484
live from Philadelphia.

183
00:06:26,664 --> 00:06:28,250
They studied the dances,

184
00:06:28,250 --> 00:06:30,298
the clothes and the hairstyles,

185
00:06:30,298 --> 00:06:32,413
and tried them out the very next

186
00:06:32,413 --> 00:06:32,678
day.

187
00:06:33,027 --> 00:06:35,832
Records were forty-five r p m singles,

188
00:06:35,832 --> 00:06:37,825
with one song on each side,

189
00:06:37,825 --> 00:06:40,260
and they cost less than a dollar.

190
00:06:40,440 --> 00:06:43,158
Teenagers kept them in a little carrying

191
00:06:43,158 --> 00:06:45,468
case and took them to each other's

192
00:06:45,468 --> 00:06:45,944
houses.

193
00:06:46,124 --> 00:06:47,014
And at night,

194
00:06:47,014 --> 00:06:49,410
they listened to rock and roll disc

195
00:06:49,410 --> 00:06:50,847
jockeys on the radio,

196
00:06:50,847 --> 00:06:54,065
sometimes from stations hundreds of miles away.

197
00:06:54,414 --> 00:06:56,435
Not every parent was happy about it.

198
00:06:56,615 --> 00:06:59,353
Many fathers still thought rock and roll

199
00:06:59,353 --> 00:07:02,023
was noise, and some preachers called it

200
00:07:02,023 --> 00:07:03,256
the devil's music.

201
00:07:03,436 --> 00:07:05,850
Kids turned the volume down when their

202
00:07:05,850 --> 00:07:06,993
parents walked by,

203
00:07:06,993 --> 00:07:09,090
and right back up when they left.

204
00:07:09,270 --> 00:07:11,405
Of course, by the end of the

205
00:07:11,405 --> 00:07:14,989
decade, even some parents were secretly humming

206
00:07:14,989 --> 00:07:15,446
along.

207
00:07:15,796 --> 00:07:18,304
Friday night meant one thing in most

208
00:07:18,304 --> 00:07:19,349
American towns.

209
00:07:19,529 --> 00:07:20,702
The football game.

210
00:07:20,882 --> 00:07:22,844
The whole town came out.

211
00:07:23,024 --> 00:07:24,228
The band played,

212
00:07:24,228 --> 00:07:26,109
the cheerleaders cheered,

213
00:07:26,109 --> 00:07:28,292
and the boys on the team were

214
00:07:28,292 --> 00:07:29,270
local heroes.

215
00:07:29,450 --> 00:07:32,216
Girls wore their boyfriend's letter sweater or

216
00:07:32,216 --> 00:07:34,141
his class ring on a chain around

217
00:07:34,141 --> 00:07:34,802
their neck.

218
00:07:35,152 --> 00:07:36,256
After the game,

219
00:07:36,256 --> 00:07:38,390
there was a dance in the gym,

220
00:07:38,390 --> 00:07:39,421
or a sock hop,

221
00:07:39,421 --> 00:07:42,438
so called because everyone took off their

222
00:07:42,438 --> 00:07:44,719
shoes to protect the gym floor.

223
00:07:44,899 --> 00:07:47,433
A local band or a record player

224
00:07:47,433 --> 00:07:48,986
supplied the music.

225
00:07:49,166 --> 00:07:51,967
Chaperones watched from the sides to make

226
00:07:51,967 --> 00:07:53,949
sure nobody danced too close.

227
00:07:54,129 --> 00:07:56,715
The slow songs were the ones everyone

228
00:07:56,715 --> 00:07:57,484
waited for.

229
00:07:57,834 --> 00:07:59,611
The dances had names.

230
00:07:59,791 --> 00:08:02,690
The stroll, where couples walked down a

231
00:08:02,690 --> 00:08:05,143
line between two rows of dancers.

232
00:08:05,323 --> 00:08:05,811
The bop.

233
00:08:05,991 --> 00:08:07,453
The hand jive.

234
00:08:07,633 --> 00:08:09,270
The bunny hop at a party.

235
00:08:09,450 --> 00:08:11,075
And for the slow songs,

236
00:08:11,075 --> 00:08:12,628
a careful little sway,

237
00:08:12,628 --> 00:08:15,170
with the boy's hand very politely on

238
00:08:15,170 --> 00:08:16,300
the girl's back.

239
00:08:16,650 --> 00:08:18,392
Saturday was for the car.

240
00:08:18,572 --> 00:08:20,501
In nineteen fifty-nine,

241
00:08:20,501 --> 00:08:23,438
a car meant everything to a teenage

242
00:08:23,438 --> 00:08:23,773
boy.

243
00:08:23,953 --> 00:08:26,006
Most couldn't afford a new one,

244
00:08:26,006 --> 00:08:27,927
so they bought an old Ford or

245
00:08:27,927 --> 00:08:30,377
Chevy for a hundred dollars and spent

246
00:08:30,377 --> 00:08:32,231
every weekend working on it.

247
00:08:32,411 --> 00:08:35,751
Lowered, painted, with loud mufflers and fuzzy

248
00:08:35,751 --> 00:08:37,856
dice hanging from the mirror.

249
00:08:38,206 --> 00:08:39,751
On Saturday nights,

250
00:08:39,751 --> 00:08:41,213
teenagers cruised.

251
00:08:41,393 --> 00:08:43,312
Up and down the main drag,

252
00:08:43,312 --> 00:08:44,419
past the diner,

253
00:08:44,419 --> 00:08:45,894
around the drive-in,

254
00:08:45,894 --> 00:08:47,001
and back again.

255
00:08:47,181 --> 00:08:49,074
Car radios blasting.

256
00:08:49,254 --> 00:08:50,438
Windows down.

257
00:08:50,618 --> 00:08:52,197
Waving at friends.

258
00:08:52,377 --> 00:08:53,647
For many teenagers,

259
00:08:53,647 --> 00:08:55,988
cruising was the whole point of the

260
00:08:55,988 --> 00:08:58,863
evening, and gasoline at about thirty cents

261
00:08:58,863 --> 00:09:00,736
a gallon made it affordable.

262
00:09:01,085 --> 00:09:02,281
And then there was the date.

263
00:09:02,461 --> 00:09:04,250
A boy would call a girl on

264
00:09:04,250 --> 00:09:05,696
the family telephone,

265
00:09:05,696 --> 00:09:07,278
usually in the hallway,

266
00:09:07,278 --> 00:09:09,067
where everyone could hear.

267
00:09:09,247 --> 00:09:11,175
He'd ask her parents' permission.

268
00:09:11,355 --> 00:09:12,999
On the night of the date,

269
00:09:12,999 --> 00:09:14,315
he came to the door,

270
00:09:14,315 --> 00:09:15,894
shook her father's hand,

271
00:09:15,894 --> 00:09:17,999
and promised to have her home by

272
00:09:17,999 --> 00:09:18,460
eleven.

273
00:09:18,809 --> 00:09:21,186
The most popular date was the drive-in

274
00:09:21,186 --> 00:09:21,561
movie.

275
00:09:21,741 --> 00:09:23,206
In nineteen fifty-nine,

276
00:09:23,206 --> 00:09:26,009
there were more than four thousand drive-ins

277
00:09:26,009 --> 00:09:26,710
in America.

278
00:09:26,890 --> 00:09:29,623
A carload of teenagers could get in

279
00:09:29,623 --> 00:09:31,185
for a dollar or two,

280
00:09:31,185 --> 00:09:34,153
and sometimes a few extra friends rode

281
00:09:34,153 --> 00:09:35,168
in the trunk.

282
00:09:35,348 --> 00:09:38,117
In fifty-nine, the movies were Some Like

283
00:09:38,117 --> 00:09:39,986
It Hot, North by Northwest,

284
00:09:39,986 --> 00:09:40,816
and Ben-Hur.

285
00:09:41,166 --> 00:09:42,585
If things got serious,

286
00:09:42,585 --> 00:09:44,197
a couple would go steady.

287
00:09:44,377 --> 00:09:46,542
The boy gave the girl his class

288
00:09:46,542 --> 00:09:48,778
ring, which she wore on a chain,

289
00:09:48,778 --> 00:09:50,943
or wrapped with tape or yarn so

290
00:09:50,943 --> 00:09:52,620
it would fit her finger.

291
00:09:52,800 --> 00:09:55,180
Going steady meant you didn't date anyone

292
00:09:55,180 --> 00:09:55,470
else.

293
00:09:55,650 --> 00:09:57,287
Many parents worried about it.

294
00:09:57,467 --> 00:09:59,497
And many of those couples ended up

295
00:09:59,497 --> 00:09:59,975
married.

296
00:10:00,324 --> 00:10:02,600
Sunday belonged to the family.

297
00:10:02,780 --> 00:10:04,743
Most teenagers went to church,

298
00:10:04,743 --> 00:10:06,640
dressed in their Sunday best,

299
00:10:06,640 --> 00:10:08,864
sitting in the same pew as always.

300
00:10:09,044 --> 00:10:10,985
Then came Sunday dinner,

301
00:10:10,985 --> 00:10:13,897
usually roast beef or fried chicken,

302
00:10:13,897 --> 00:10:16,404
with mashed potatoes and gravy.

303
00:10:16,584 --> 00:10:18,349
Many stores were closed.

304
00:10:18,529 --> 00:10:20,456
The whole town slowed down.

305
00:10:20,806 --> 00:10:24,557
Sunday evening, the family watched television together.

306
00:10:24,737 --> 00:10:26,374
The Ed Sullivan Show,

307
00:10:26,374 --> 00:10:29,414
Lassie, Maverick, or Dennis the Menace,

308
00:10:29,414 --> 00:10:31,285
which started that fall.

309
00:10:31,465 --> 00:10:33,926
Then homework, finished at the last minute

310
00:10:33,926 --> 00:10:35,157
at the kitchen table.

311
00:10:35,337 --> 00:10:36,218
And then bed,

312
00:10:36,218 --> 00:10:37,982
because Monday was coming,

313
00:10:37,982 --> 00:10:40,491
and the whole week would start again.

314
00:10:40,841 --> 00:10:43,848
Teenagers in fifty-nine had their own language.

315
00:10:44,028 --> 00:10:46,141
A cool guy was a real gone

316
00:10:46,141 --> 00:10:46,467
cat.

317
00:10:46,647 --> 00:10:48,562
Something great was the most.

318
00:10:48,742 --> 00:10:51,522
A boring person was a square,

319
00:10:51,522 --> 00:10:52,481
or a drip.

320
00:10:52,661 --> 00:10:54,165
Your car was your wheels,

321
00:10:54,165 --> 00:10:55,429
a fight was a rumble,

322
00:10:55,429 --> 00:10:57,054
and if you liked something,

323
00:10:57,054 --> 00:10:58,559
you might say you dug it.

324
00:10:58,739 --> 00:11:00,882
Parents had no idea what you were

325
00:11:00,882 --> 00:11:01,792
talking about.

326
00:11:02,141 --> 00:11:04,336
And some things were brand new that

327
00:11:04,336 --> 00:11:04,649
year.

328
00:11:04,829 --> 00:11:07,147
In March of nineteen fifty-nine,

329
00:11:07,147 --> 00:11:09,031
a new doll went on sale at

330
00:11:09,031 --> 00:11:11,493
the American Toy Fair in New York.

331
00:11:11,673 --> 00:11:12,892
Her name was Barbie.

332
00:11:13,072 --> 00:11:16,180
In August, Hawaii became the fiftieth state,

333
00:11:16,180 --> 00:11:18,158
and the flag got a new star.

334
00:11:18,338 --> 00:11:20,911
And everyone was talking about the space

335
00:11:20,911 --> 00:11:22,970
race and seven brave astronauts.

336
00:11:23,319 --> 00:11:27,033
In April, NASA introduced the Mercury Seven,

337
00:11:27,033 --> 00:11:29,566
the first American astronauts.

338
00:11:29,746 --> 00:11:31,993
Their pictures were on the cover of

339
00:11:31,993 --> 00:11:32,892
Life magazine.

340
00:11:33,072 --> 00:11:34,543
Boys wanted to be them,

341
00:11:34,543 --> 00:11:36,462
and girls wanted to meet them.

342
00:11:36,642 --> 00:11:39,360
For teenagers in nineteen fifty-nine,

343
00:11:39,360 --> 00:11:41,563
the future was in outer space,

344
00:11:41,563 --> 00:11:43,399
and it seemed very close.

345
00:11:43,750 --> 00:11:44,876
Was it all fun?

346
00:11:45,056 --> 00:11:45,288
No.

347
00:11:45,468 --> 00:11:48,986
Teenagers in fifty-nine faced plenty of pressure.

348
00:11:49,166 --> 00:11:51,364
Boys knew that the draft was waiting

349
00:11:51,364 --> 00:11:52,463
after high school.

350
00:11:52,643 --> 00:11:55,023
Girls felt pressure to marry early.

351
00:11:55,203 --> 00:11:57,560
Many families had strict rules.

352
00:11:57,740 --> 00:12:00,198
And for Black teenagers in the South,

353
00:12:00,198 --> 00:12:03,453
schools and soda fountains were still segregated,

354
00:12:03,453 --> 00:12:05,579
and the fight to change that was

355
00:12:05,579 --> 00:12:06,575
just beginning.

356
00:12:06,925 --> 00:12:07,869
But for many,

357
00:12:07,869 --> 00:12:10,993
being a teenager in nineteen fifty-nine was

358
00:12:10,993 --> 00:12:11,429
magic.

359
00:12:11,609 --> 00:12:13,404
There was a feeling of possibility in

360
00:12:13,404 --> 00:12:13,792
the air.

361
00:12:13,972 --> 00:12:16,073
The country was rich and growing.

362
00:12:16,253 --> 00:12:18,006
Jobs were easy to find.

363
00:12:18,186 --> 00:12:19,516
The music was new,

364
00:12:19,516 --> 00:12:21,288
the cars were beautiful,

365
00:12:21,288 --> 00:12:22,691
and you were young.

366
00:12:22,871 --> 00:12:24,459
It was a good time to be

367
00:12:24,459 --> 00:12:25,716
sixteen in America.

368
00:12:26,066 --> 00:12:28,372
If you were a teenager in fifty-nine,

369
00:12:28,372 --> 00:12:30,617
you're in your early eighties today.

370
00:12:30,797 --> 00:12:32,844
You lived through the sixties,

371
00:12:32,844 --> 00:12:35,232
the seventies and everything after.

372
00:12:35,412 --> 00:12:36,766
But somewhere inside,

373
00:12:36,766 --> 00:12:38,960
there's still that kid at the soda

374
00:12:38,960 --> 00:12:41,734
fountain, dropping a quarter in the jukebox

375
00:12:41,734 --> 00:12:43,991
and waiting for your favorite song.

376
00:12:44,342 --> 00:12:46,106
So tell us in the comments.

377
00:12:46,286 --> 00:12:48,353
Where did you go on Saturday nights

378
00:12:48,353 --> 00:12:49,711
in nineteen fifty-nine?

379
00:12:49,891 --> 00:12:51,561
What was your favorite song,

380
00:12:51,561 --> 00:12:53,409
and who did you go steady with?

381
00:12:53,589 --> 00:12:55,005
We read every comment.

382
00:12:55,185 --> 00:12:57,706
Next time, we're looking at the rise

383
00:12:57,706 --> 00:12:59,806
and fall of the Sears catalog.

384
00:12:59,986 --> 00:13:01,769
Thank you for spending this time with

385
00:13:01,769 --> 00:13:01,913
me.
